# Untitled

## Flexible-on-rigid heteroepitaxial metal-organic frameworks induced by template lattice change
XiaoGang Wang, Han Cheng§ (✉), and XianZheng Zhang§ (✉)
Key Laboratory of Biomedical Polymers of Ministry of Education & Department of Chemistry, Wuhan University, Wuhan 430072, China § Han Cheng and XianZheng Zhang contributed equally to this work.
© Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2021 Received: 2 September 2021 / Revised: 14 November 2021 / Accepted: 19 November 2021
## ABSTRACT
Hybrid-phase metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of intriguing heterostructures for diverse applications, the properties of which are governed by their chemical composition, framework topology, and morphology. Herein, we report the structural and morphological evolution of flexible MOFs induced by the lattice change of template during heteroepitaxial growth. We demonstrate that the epitaxially grown flexible Fe-MOFs can be varied from one structure to another to adapt to the lattice of the template Zr-MOFs. Thus, flexible Fe-MOFs with similar chemical compositions and topology can be epitaxially grown on different Zr-MOFs over huge lattice constant gradient. We also demonstrate that the morphology of the heterostructures is affected by the degree of lattice difference between the template MOFs and the epitaxial MOFs. The reported results could pave the way toward the rational design of hybrid-phase MOFs guided by the principles of reticular chemistry.
## KEYWORDS
metal-organic framework (MOF), heteroepitaxial growth, MOF-on-MOF, structural evolution, heterostructure
## 1 Introduction
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), also known as porous coordination polymers (PCP), have drawn much attention for many emerging applications due to their structural and functional accuracy and feasibility [1]. Hybrid-phase MOFs are a class of intriguing heterostructures assembled by more than two different MOFs. Compared with single-phase MOFs, the inherent heterogeneity and structure diversity of hybrid-phase MOFs enable the integration of multiple properties and provide more regulatory factors for the design of multifunctional materials [2–5]. The functions of hybrid-phase MOFs are primarily determined by their chemical composition and framework topology, and are also affected by their morphology [6–10]. Therefore, precise control of the chemical composition and framework topology on the molecular scale, and adjustment of the morphology on the mesoscale, are both very important for designing hybrid-phase MOFs with application-targeted properties.
A well-known approach of constructing hybrid-phase MOFs is to epitaxially grow a secondary MOF on a template MOF with well-matched topology and lattice constant. The conformal overgrowth of MOFs with perfectly matched lattice leads to the hybrid-phase MOFs with a core–shell architecture [11–13]. A few recent works reported the conjugation of different MOFs with diverse topologies and lattice constants by oriented heteroepitaxial growth [14–19]. The theoretical basis for the oriented heteroepitaxial growth is that even if two crystals have dissimilar topologies and lattice constants, some of their crystallographic planes still may be matched to form a defect-free interface for the
phase transition from one crystal phase to another [14, 17, 20]. Kim and coworkers screened out six MOF pairs that can be connected to form single crystal MOF@MOF heterostructure through a joint computational/experimental approach [17]. Oh and coworkers prepared an unusual semitubular shaped hybrid-phase MOF by the anisotropic growth of MOF-NDC (NDC standing for naphthalene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid) on the ac plane of MIL-68 [15]. Zhao and coworkers recently constructed a series of MOF heterostructures with atypical morphology by selective epitaxial growth, including nanorods-on-ellipsoid, nanorods-on-nanorods, nanorods-on-nanosheet, and nanosheet-on-nanosheet [18]. Recently, our group constructed a class of flexible-on-rigid hybrid-phase MOFs with unique rod-on-octahedron morphology [19]. However, in order to constructing hybrid-phase MOFs with multi-level and sophisticated control of chemical composition, framework topology and morphology, deeper insights of the oriented epitaxial growth need to be revealed.
In our previous work, we described the construction mechanism of the flexible-on-rigid hybrid-phase MOFs. In this work we have taken a step forward for the construction of flexible-on-rigid hybrid MOFs with distinct lattice parameters. We reported herein the structural and morphological evolution of epitaxial grown flexible MOFs induced by the lattice change of template MOFs during the construction of flexible-on-rigid hybrid-phase MOFs. The results in this work indicated that the flexible Fe-BDC (BDC standing 1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid) and Fe-NDC epitaxially grown on Zr-MOFs can be varied from one structure to another to adapt to the lattice of the template. With the template lattice adaptive structural variation, flexible Fe-BDC and Fe-NDC can be epitaxially grown on Zr-MOFs with huger lattice constant
gradients (presented in Scheme 1), compared with previous work. In addition, in this work we also found that the change of the template lattice could affect the spatial arrangement of the epitaxial flexible MOFs, thereby affecting the morphology of heterostructures.
## 2 Results and discussion
