The crystal structures of the heterostructures were characterized by powder X–ray diffraction (PXRD). A two-phase LeBail fitting of the PXRD patterns indicate that all these heterostructures are composed of a cubic phase and a hexagonal phase (Figs. 2(a)–2(c)). The cubic phase is the template \( Z\mathbf{r} \)-MOF, and the hexagonal phase is the epitaxially grown Fe-MOF. According to the cell parameters determined by LeBail fitting, the Fe-BDC epitaxially grown on MOF-801 and UIO-66 has close cell parameters \( (a\,=\,b\,=\,12.789(1)\,\mathrm{~\AA~} \), \( c\,=\,18.305(2)\,\mathrm{~\AA~} \) or \( a\,=\,b\,=\,12.768(1)\,\mathrm{~\AA~} \), \( c\,=\,18.348(1)\,\mathrm{~\AA~} \), space group of \( P{-}62c \), while the cell parameters of Fe-BDC epitaxially grown on DUT-52 are completely different from them \( (a=b=14.496(2)\,\mathrm{\AA}, \) \( c=17.128(5)\,\mathrm{\AA} \), space group of \( P{-}62c \). To analyze the structure of the epitaxial grown Fe-MOFs, phase pure MIL-88B and MOF-235, two known hexagonal Fe-BDC frameworks were synthesized (see field-emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM) and structure analysis in Fig. S2 in the ESM), and their PXRD patterns were compared with the heterostructures. As shown in Figs. 2(d)–2(h), the PXRD patterns of Fe-BDC-on-MOF-801 and Fe-BDC-on-UIO-66 show two sets of characteristic peaks of both template \( Z\mathbf{r} \)-MOFs (MOF-801 or UIO-66) and MOF-235, while Fe-BDC-on-DUT-52 shows the characteristic peaks of DUT-52 and MIL-88B. Therefore, the Fe-BDC epitaxially grown on MOF-801 or UIO-66 is MOF-235, and the Fe-BDC framework epitaxially grown on DUT-52 is MIL-88B.
To monitor the formation process of these MOF-on-MOF
heterostructures with different morphologies, a small portion of the reactants was collected after different times of reaction, and the samples were observed by FESEM (Fig. 3). As shown in Fig. 3, the epitaxial growth of Fe-BDC on MOF-801, UIO-66, and DUT-52 starts with heterogeneous nucleation on the surface of the octahedral template MOFs. The nucleation of Fe-BDC is the fastest on MOF-801, second on DUT-52, and slowest on UIO-66. After \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) of reaction, a large number of densely arranged FeBDC nuclei can be observed on the surface of MOF-801 and DUT-52, of which the Fe-BDC nuclei on MOF-801 have a larger size. In contrast, only a few Fe-BDC nuclei can be observed on the surface of UIO-66 after \(30\,\mathrm{\min}\) of reaction. After nucleation, Fe-BDC continues to grow epitaxially perpendicular to each face of the octahedral Zr-MOF (i.e., which is the (111) plane of the Zr-MOF). With the progress of the epitaxial growth, the densely arranged Fe
BDC nuclei on MOF-801 and DUT-52 gradually fuse into eight large crystals, thus forming atypical particles containing eight precisely aligned protrusions. The epitaxial growth of Fe-BDC on UIO-66 is quite different from the above situations. After \(8\ \mathrm{h}\) of reaction, although some of the epitaxial Fe-BDC on UIO-66 have grown to a larger size, there are still some exposed UIO-66 crystal planes available for the nucleation of Fe-BDC, resulting in a big size difference of the Fe-BDC epitaxially grown on UIO-66.
MOF-235 and MIL-88B are structural identical frameworks with the same flexible acs topology, except that MOF-235 contained \(\mathrm{FeCl_{4}^{-}}\) in the pores as the counter anions, leading to their different swelling properties (Figs. 4(a) and 4(b)) [30–33]. Based on the LeBail fitting of their PXRD patterns (Fig. S3 in the ESM), the cell parameters within the (001) plane of MOF-235 and MIL-88B immersed in DMF are \(a=b=1.0654(1)\ \mathrm{\AA}\) and \(a=b=\)
14.375(1) Å, respectively. According to our previous work, the (111) plane of fcu net and the (001) plane of acs net are topologically matched to fcu in a defect-free interface for the
construction of acs-on-fcu hybrid frameworks (Fig. 4(c)) [19]. Therefore, if the lattice constants are also matched, both MIL-88B and MOF-235 can be epitaxially grown on the (111) plane of Zr-
MOFs with fcu topology. To epitaxially grow on MOF-801, UIO-66, and DUT-52, MOF-235 and MIL-88B need to adapt their lattices to \( a = b = 12.617, 14.670, \) and \( 16.907 \, \text{\AA} \), respectively (Fig. 4(d)), measured from previously reported structure) [23, 34, 35]. Although MOF-235 and MIL-88B have flexible topologies, there are still energy barriers to change their lattices from the intrinsic lattice of the lowest energy state to the lattice matching the template Zr-MOFs (abbreviated as \( \Delta E_{\text{MOF-235 lattice}} \) and \( \Delta E_{\text{MIL-88B lattice}} \) respectively). In addition, the reactions of \( \text{FeCl}_3 \) and BDC in solution to form MOF-235 phase or MIL-88B phase have to overcome a bulk energy barrier, which are abbreviated as \( \Delta E_{\text{MOF-235 bulk}} \) and \( \Delta E_{\text{MIL-88B bulk}} \) respectively. Without the addition of template, the product of the reaction between \( \text{FeCl}_3 \) and BDC at \( 105 \, \text{°C} \) in DMF is MOF-235 (with some MIL-101 as by-product, see PXRD patterns in Fig. S4 in the ESM). Therefore, under the same reaction condition as that for the epitaxial growth, MOF-235 is the preferred product compared to MIL-88B, namely, \( \Delta E_{\text{MOF-235 bulk}} < \Delta E_{\text{MIL-88B bulk}} \).
With the above knowledge, we analyzed the epitaxial growth process of Fe-BDC on different Zr-MOFs from the perspective of the reaction energy barrier (Fig. 4(d)). When MOF-801 is used as the template, the lattice parameters of Fe-BDC required for epitaxial growth \( (a = b = 12.617 \, \text{\AA}) \) are very close to the intrinsic lattice parameters of MOF-235 \( (a = b = 12.654 \, \text{\AA}) \), but quite different from those of MIL-88B \( (a = b = 14.375 \, \text{\AA}) \). Therefore, the energy barrier of MOF-235 to adapt to the lattice of MOF-801 is lower than that of MIL-88B (i.e., \( \Delta E_{\text{MOF-235 lattice}} < \Delta E_{\text{MIL-88B lattice}} \)). In addition, as described above, the bulk energy barrier for the formation MOF-235 phase is also lower than that of MIL-88B (i.e., \( \Delta E_{\text{MOF-235 bulk}} < \Delta E_{\text{MIL-88B bulk}} \)). Both of the above factors are conducive to the formation of MOF-235-on-MOF-801 rather than MIL-88B-on-MOF-801. When UIO-66 is used as the template, the lattice parameters of Fe-BDC required for epitaxial growth \( (a = b = 14.670 \, \text{\AA}) \) are close to the intrinsic lattice parameters of MIL-88B \( (a = b = 14.375 \, \text{\AA}) \), but quite different from those of MOF-235 \( (a = b = 12.654 \, \text{\AA}) \), (i.e., \( \Delta E_{\text{MOF-235 lattice}} > \)
\( \Delta E_{\text{MIL-88B lattice}} \)). However, probably due to the high bulk energy barrier of MIL-88B has hindered the epitaxial growth of MIL-88B, thus, MOF-235-on-UIO-66 is formed, even though its formation needs to overcome the energy barrier caused by the large lattice difference between UIO-66 and MOF-235. Nevertheless, the large lattice difference has led to the slow nucleation rate of MOF-235 on the surface of UIO-66. When DUT-52 is used as the template, the huge lattice difference between MOF-235 and DUT-52 (12.654 Å vs. 16.907 Å) hinders the nucleation of MOF-235, thus MIL-88B-on-DUT-52 is the formed, despite the relatively high bulk energy barrier of MIL-88B, and the lattice difference between MIL-88B and DUT-52 (14.375 Å vs. 16.907 Å). The homogenous nucleation of Fe-BDC (which will lead to a mixture of Fe-BDC and Zr-MOF) needs to overcome a high surface energy barrier and also the bulk energy barrier of the Fe-BDC. The successful formation of MIL-88B-on-DUT-52 indicates that the overall energy barrier for the construction of MIL-88B-on-DUT-52 is lower than the free energy barrier of homogenous nucleation. Note that, there is a limitation of the reaction energy barrier model in the above analysis, that is, the lattice expansion of flexible Fe-BDC/Fe-NDC in DMF with the increase of temperature has not been considered. In this work, all the lattice parameters of flexible Fe-BDC and Fe-NDC in DMF were based on the PXRD pattern in room temperature. Based on previous report, the lattice of flexible MIL-88 series MOFs expanded with the increase of temperature [33]. Since the construction of Fe-MOFs-on-Zr-MOFs in this work is at \( 105 \, \text{°C} \), the lattice change required of MIL-88B during formation MIL-88B-on-DUT-52 at \( 105 \, \text{°C} \) will be lower than that at room temperature. The unconsidered lattice expansion of MIL-88B in DMF at high temperature could help explain the fact that the nucleation of MIL-88B on DUT-52 is quite fast (Fig. 3).
To verify the template lattice scale induced structure change is not a unique phenomenon of Fe-BDC, we tried the epitaxial growth of Fe-NDC, which is an isoreticular structure of Fe-BDC with longer linker. To our surprise, Fe-NDC can be epitaxially
grown on Zr-MOFs with an unexpectedly huge lattice constant gradient, including MOF-801, UIO-66, DUT-52, UIO-67, and BUT-30. To epitaxially grow on these Zr-MOFs, Fe-NDC needs to adapt cell parameter to \( a = b = 12.617 \), 14.670, 16.907, 18.928. and 21.357 Å, respectively (Scheme 1). PXRD results indicated that the epitaxial Fe-NDC also undergoes a structural variation with the increase of template lattice (Figs. 5(a)–5(f), see LeBail fittings of the PXRD patterns in Figs. S5–S10 in the ESM). The epitaxial growth of Fe-NDC on MOF-801 forms an analogue of MIL-88C with cell parameters of \( a = b = 12.792(1) \) Å, \( c = 22.975(3) \) Å. Interestingly, the epitaxial growth of Fe-NDC on UIO-66 could lead to two different structures by simply changing the reaction temperature, which are MIL-88C \( (a = b = 18.933(2) \) Å, \( c = 18.586(2) \) Å, reaction at \( 100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) ) and the same MIL-88C analogues as that on MOF-801 \( (a = b = 12.814(1) \) Å, \( c = 22.982(4) \) Å, reaction at \( 145\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) ). Due to the large difference between the lattice required for epitaxial growth on UIO-66 \( (a = b = 14.670 \) Å) and the intrinsic lattice of the two structures \( (a = b = 18.933 \) or 12.814 Å), these two heterostructures with the same composition of Fe-NDC-on-UIO-66 but different structures have irregular morphology (Figs. 5(h) and 5(i)). The structures of Fe-NDC epitaxially grown on DUT-52, UIO-67, and BUT-30 are all MIL-88C (see PXRD of MIL-88C in Fig. S11 in the ESM). Overall, 6 kinds of heterostructures with different chemical composition, framework structures, and morphologies were obtained by epitaxially growing Fe-NDC on different Zr-MOFs.
## 3 Conclusions
In summary, 9 kinds of MOF-on-MOF heterostructures with different chemical composition, framework topology, and morphology were synthesized and analyzed. We have discovered that the lattice change of template can affect the structure and morphology of the epitaxially grown flexible MOFs during the heteroepitaxial growth process. We demonstrated that the reason for the structural variation of the epitaxial flexible MOFs is to adapt to the template lattice, thereby reducing the energy barrier caused by lattice difference. With the lattice adaptive structural variation, flexible MOFs with similar chemical compositions and topology can be epitaxially grown on template MOFs with huge lattice scale gradients. For example, Fe-NDC can be epitaxial on different Zr-MOFs with unexpected huge lattice scale gradients (from 12.617 to 21.357 Å). The template lattice adaptive structure variation during epitaxial growth described herein could be extended to the heteroepitaxial growth of other flexible crystals (not only MOFs), with the prerequisites that the epitaxial grown structure is at least partially topologically matched to the template at the interface. The results described in this work will lead to a deeper understanding of the epitaxial MOF-on-MOF heterostructures, which could accelerate their pace towards real-life applications.
